# Chapter 3: a service goes down, the order waits, the service comes back
#
# What happens to an order when service B is down? A publishes, nobody acks, the order stays PUBLISHED. The event is not lost:
# it waits in B's queue, and when B is back it acks. This chapter stops B with a daemon operation, watches the order wait,
# starts B again and watches the late ack arrive. It ends with B running, as it found it.
# @env local
# @var corr = {{correlation}}
# @var msg = ord-{{uuid}}

### op service.stop stop-b
# `service.stop` stops B's container and keeps it (the IDE's Services tab has the same Stop button).
# @assert $.status == stopped
{"name": "b"}

### sqs send queue/inbox send-order
# @attr id={{msg}}
# @attr correlation={{corr}}
# @body ../estate/payloads/01-simple-order.json

### dynamodb get table/messages stored
# @until $.status.S == RECEIVED within 20s
{"Key": {"id": {"S": "{{msg}}"}}}

### http publish
# @assert status == 200
POST {{service/a}}/publish/{{msg}}
X-Correlation-Id: {{corr}}

### wait b-is-down
# Give B the time it would need to ack (it would take well under a second).
# @for 5s

### dynamodb get table/messages still-published
# Nobody acked: the order is still PUBLISHED. (Chapter 2's `acked` step would fail here after 30 s; in a scenario a
# failed check never stops the run, every later step still runs and the result shows exactly which link broke.)
# @assert $.status.S == PUBLISHED
{"Key": {"id": {"S": "{{msg}}"}}}

### op service.start start-b
# B starts again (the same container) and waits until healthy.
# @assert $.status == running
{"name": "b"}

### dynamodb get table/messages late-ack
# B reads the event that waited in its queue and acks: the order becomes ACKED, late.
# @until $.status.S == ACKED within 120s
{"Key": {"id": {"S": "{{msg}}"}}}

### op logs.fetch a-log
# A `logs` step listens while the run goes (chapter 2); after the fact, `logs.fetch` reads a log into the session as a file
# (sessions/<session>/logs/). Filtered by this order's correlation id, A's account of it now ends with the late "ACK received".
# @assert $.lines >= 3
{"target": "log/service-a", "since": "10m", "filter": "{{corr}}"}

# In the IDE: Runs of This Scenario… on chapter 2, pick a run, "Diff with..." and a run of this chapter would compare
# different scenarios; compare instead two runs of estate/scenarios/order.scn, one while B is stopped: the diff shows exactly
# the steps and log lines that the outage changed.
